the existing Kueue integration\n42\t  (per architecture context). Validation of PyTorchJob behavior under gang\n43\t  admission and preemption becomes acceptance criteria on the Distributed\n44\t  Workloads epics (same owning team, so no separate validation epic).\n45\t- **Dashboard** — active (HLR-3 visibility). Generates an epic owned by the\n46\t  RHOAI Dashboard team.\n47\t\n48\t## Investigation Analysis (Step 3)\n49\t\n50\tThe strategy's three open questions were each evaluated against the decision\n51\trule *\"does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?\"*\n52\t\n53\t| Open question | Gates | Verdict |\n54\t|---|---|---|\n55\t| 1. Do Kueue's gang-admission semantics (waitForPodsReady) satisfy HLR-1 for PyTorchJob/RayJob at target scale, or is scheduler-plugin work needed? | HLR-1 implementation | **Investigation (E001)**. The strategy states the answer \"changes the shape of most implementation work\". Two bounded outcomes → conditional branches (A: Kueue as-is, B: scheduler-plugin/coscheduling). Note: HLR-2 (quota enforcement) is *not* gated — Kueue's ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota model is GA per architecture context and enforces quota at admission regardless of which gang-scheduling mechanism is selected, so the quota epic proceeds identically under either outcome. |\n56\t| 2. What preemption semantics do reference customers need — full eviction with requeue, or checkpoint-aware graceful preemption? | HLR-4 implementation | **Investigation (E002)**. Conflicting field signals + missing checkpoint-overhead benchmark; the answer rewrites the preemption epic's scope. Two bounded outcomes, but the preemption implementation epic exists under either outcome (HLR-4 asks for admin-defined policy; either semantics is a configuration of it) → modeled as a gated main-plan epic with `rewrite` impact rather than branches. |\n57\t| 3. Is Tech Preview node topology discovery mature enough to build HLR-5 on this release? | HLR-5 implementation | **Investigation (E003)**. If immature, HLR-5 is deferred and its epic does not exist this release → gated main-plan epic with `remove` impact. |\n58\t\n59\tNo other unknowns appear in the strategy (no risks table, assumptions, pending\n60\treviews, or conditional ADRs are present). Implementation details the strategy\n61\tleaves open (exact quota provisioning model, Dashboard view layout, preemption\n62\tpolicy schema) are captured as acceptance criteria on the relevant epics, not\n63\tflagged.\n64\t\n65\t## Epic List (main plan)\n66\t\n67\t| ID | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority |\n68\t|----|-------|------|-----------|------|----------|\n69\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E001 | Validate Kueue gang admission for multi-node training jobs at target scale | Investigation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n70\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E002 | Determine required preemption semantics for training workloads | Investigation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n71\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E003 | Assess maturity of node topology discovery for topology-aware placement | Investigation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P2 |\n72\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E004 | Enforce per-project GPU quota at admission for training workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n73\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E005 | Surface queue position, quota consumption, and admission state in the Dashboard | Implementation | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n74\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E006 | Implement admin-defined preemption policy for training workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n75\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E007 | Implement topology-aware GPU placement preference | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | P2 |\n76\t\n77\t### Conditional branch epics (gated by E001, not standalone Jira issues)\n78\t\n79\t| ID | Title | Type | Component | Team | Priority |\n80\t|----|-------|------|-----------|------